{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "PR Review Analyzer",
  "description": "Fetches open pull requests, analyzes code review comments for validity, presents a report, offers fix options, and posts replies to resolved conversations via GitHub MCP.",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "userTriggered"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "The user wants to analyze code review comments on an open pull request and respond to them.\n\nCRITICAL: You MUST use GitHub MCP tools for ALL GitHub operations. Do NOT use webFetch, remote_web_search, or any web-based tools to access GitHub content.\n\nCRITICAL: Do NOT read the hook file (.kiro/hooks/pr-review-analyzer.kiro.hook) at any point during this workflow. Reading the hook file causes the agent session to hang.\n\nNOTE: Replies to PR review conversations are posted using mcp_github_add_reply_to_pull_request_comment, which is already auto-approved in the GitHub MCP config.\n\n---\n\nStep 1 — Discover the repository and open PRs automatically.\nUse mcp_github_get_me to get the authenticated user's login. Then use mcp_github_search_pull_requests with query 'author:{login} is:open' to find all of the user's open PRs. The repo owner and name can be extracted directly from the PR search results. If PRs exist in multiple repos, present the list grouped by repo and ask the user which PR to analyze (show PR number, title, repo, and author). If there is exactly one open PR, confirm with the user before proceeding. If there are no open PRs, inform the user and stop.\n\nStep 2 — Fetch review comments.\nUse the appropriate GitHub MCP read tools to fetch all review comments on the selected PR. Collect: comment ID, the file path and line the comment is anchored to, the comment body, and the author.\n\nStep 3 — Read the relevant source files.\nFor each comment that references a specific file and line, use mcp_github_get_file_contents to read the current content of that file from the PR branch so you have full context for your analysis.\n\nStep 4 — Analyze each comment.\nFor each review comment, evaluate:\n- Is the concern technically valid given the code at that location?\n- Does it conflict with or align with the project's steering rules (security, logging, auth, backend/frontend standards, packaging)?\n- Is it a style/preference comment, a correctness issue, a security concern, or a false positive?\n- Assign a verdict: VALID_CRITICAL | VALID_WARNING | PREFERENCE | FALSE_POSITIVE\n- Write a one-sentence rationale for the verdict.\n\nStep 5 — Present the analysis report.\nOutput a structured report in this format:\n\n  PR REVIEW ANALYSIS REPORT — [repo] #[PR number]\n\n  Comment #[id] — [file]:[line] — [author]\n  Code:    [relevant code snippet]\n  Comment: [comment body]\n  Verdict: [VALID_CRITICAL | VALID_WARNING | PREFERENCE | FALSE_POSITIVE]\n  Reason:  [one-sentence rationale]\n\n  ---\n  Summary: [X] critical, [Y] warnings, [Z] preferences, [W] false positives\n\nStep 6 — Offer fix options for valid concerns.\nFor each comment with verdict VALID_CRITICAL or VALID_WARNING, ask the user (via userInput tool) whether to:\n  - Apply fix automatically (Kiro will make the code change)\n  - Skip this comment\n  - Mark as acknowledged (will reply but not change code)\n\nPresent all valid concerns together so the user can make choices in one pass before any changes are made.\n\nStep 7 — Apply selected fixes.\nFor each comment the user chose to fix, make the minimal code change needed to address the concern. Follow all project steering rules (security, logging, auth, backend/frontend standards). After all fixes are applied, summarize what was changed.\n\nStep 8 — Test and commit the fixes.\nAfter applying all fixes:\n  - Run getDiagnostics on all modified files to check for syntax, linting, type, and semantic issues\n  - If diagnostics reveal errors, fix them before proceeding\n  - Run the appropriate test suite for the modified code, use steering guidelines to determine the test suite.\n  - If tests fail, fix the issues before proceeding\n  - Once all checks pass, commit the changes:\n    - Stage all changes: git add .\n    - Commit with message: 'fix: Address PR review comments - [brief summary]'\n    - Verify commit: git log --oneline -1\n  - Present a summary: files changed, tests passed, commit SHA\n\nStep 9 — Post replies to PR review conversations.\nFor each comment that was analyzed, post a reply using mcp_github_add_reply_to_pull_request_comment:\n  - VALID_CRITICAL / VALID_WARNING (fixed): 'Fixed — [brief description of what was changed].'\n  - VALID_CRITICAL / VALID_WARNING (acknowledged, not fixed): 'Acknowledged — [brief explanation].'\n  - PREFERENCE: 'Noted — this is a style preference. No change made.'\n  - FALSE_POSITIVE: 'This appears to be a false positive — [brief rationale]. No change made.'\n\nUse mcp_github_add_reply_to_pull_request_comment for each reply.\n\nStep 10 — Final summary.\nReport: how many comments were analyzed, how many fixes were applied, how many replies were posted, and whether any critical issues remain unresolved."
  },
  "workspaceFolderName": "se-ise-t-kiro-governance",
  "shortName": "pr-review-analyzer"
}